"""The model-family registry: one entry per trained model/scaler pair this project
evaluates, plus the dataset and feature-prep kind each needs.

Moved verbatim out of Evaluations.ipynb cell 2, where it was a 56,914-character
dict literal -- 96.6% of that cell. No generator replaces it. 38 of 50 entries
follow the exact template `model_year_{year}_<family_id>.pkl`, but the 12
exceptions span four unrelated kinds (two legacy untemplated families, one
null-scaler rbf family, nine mlp_combined_* with a stripped 'mlp_' prefix), and
that same prefix rule is applied inconsistently: all 44 families with a
final_model_path strip it, but the 26 experience_replay families do not apply it
to model_template while the 9 mlp_combined_* families do. A generator would have
to encode that contradiction as a flag justified only by "that's what's on disk."
The literal is ground truth about the filesystem; when a filename is wrong you
want FileNotFoundError, not a wrong generator argument.

`short_label` is new here. It replaces a second, drift-prone registry that lived
in the reporting notebook cell: a 38-entry `row_label_renames` dict (9 of which
mapped a key to itself) covering only 38 of the 50 families, with the other 12
silently falling through to their raw family_id in published matrices. Every
family now carries its own display label -- the 38 that had one keep their exact
existing value (including a `missclassification` -> `misclassification` typo
fix), and the 12 that didn't get their raw family_id, reproducing today's
fall-through exactly. A family added without a label is now a build-time
KeyError-shaped omission you'd notice, not a raw id quietly appearing in a report.
"""

